          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Crossed complexes
            (of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizontal+categorification"} many object
            generalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
          %p
            Crossed complexes are also an equivalent encoding of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} strict ∞-groupoids
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 2
            are the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source"} source
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibers"} fibers
            of the collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-morphism"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              \-morphisms
            of the strict
            = succeed "-groupoid," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+action"} acted on
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            in degrees
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq 1
          %p
            Later on we will look in a bit more detail at the fundamental crossed complex of a filtered space, and that is a good example to keep in mind. For simplicity assume we have a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-complex"} CW-complex
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X_1,X_0)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2(X_2,X_1,x)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            we get a family of groups which we think of as a groupoid having just vertex groups without any arrows joining distinct vertices. In the next dimension we have
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 3
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_3(X_3,X_2,x)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X_1,X_0)
            on all of these. It was studying these groups , actions etc. that gave the abstract definition that follows.
          %h2#definition Definition
